#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:1
msgid "Shared Memory and Synchronization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:3
msgid ""
"Every thread in a CUDA thread block has access to a small, fast scratch-pad "
"called **shared memory**. It sits on-chip next to the SM's execution units — "
"roughly 100× faster than global memory and roughly 10× faster than the L1 "
"cache. The catch is size: typically 48–228 KB per SM depending on "
"architecture, shared across all blocks running on that SM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:9
msgid ""
"cuda-oxide exposes shared memory through `SharedArray` and "
"`DynamicSharedArray`, both designed to feel like Rust arrays while compiling "
"to PTX address space 3. This chapter covers how to use them, when to "
"synchronize, and the tiling pattern that turns naive kernels into fast ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:15
msgid ""
"[CUDA Programming Guide — Shared Memory](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-"
"programming-guide/#shared-memory) for hardware details on bank structure, "
"broadcast rules, and capacity per architecture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:22
msgid "Why shared memory matters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:24
msgid ""
"Consider the naive GEMM from the [Async MLP Pipeline](../projects/async-mlp-"
"pipeline.md) project. Each thread computes one output element by reading an "
"entire row of A and an entire column of B from global memory. For a 64×64 "
"matrix, that is 128 global loads *per thread*, and every other thread in the "
"block loads many of the same elements. The hardware dutifully fetches each "
"one from DRAM — or, if you are lucky, the L2 cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:32
msgid ""
"Shared memory changes the economics. A thread block cooperatively loads a "
"**tile** of A and a tile of B into shared memory, synchronizes, and then "
"every thread reads from the tile. Each global load is amortized across all "
"threads that reuse the element. For a 16×16 tile, that is a 16× reduction in "
"global memory traffic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:42
msgid ""
"The tiled computation pattern. Threads cooperatively load tiles of A and B "
"from global memory into `SharedArray`s, synchronize, compute partial "
"products from the fast on-chip memory, synchronize again, and repeat for the "
"next tile along the K dimension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:50
msgid "SharedArray — static shared memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:52
msgid ""
"`SharedArray<T, N, ALIGN>` is a fixed-size array allocated in shared memory "
"at compile time. You declare it as a `static mut` inside the kernel:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:55
msgid ""
"use cuda_device::thread::Runtime2DIndex;\n"
"use cuda_device::{DisjointSlice, SharedArray, kernel, thread};\n"
"\n"
"const TILE: usize = 16;\n"
"\n"
"#[kernel]\n"
"pub fn tiled_sgemm(\n"
"    m: u32, n: u32, k: u32,\n"
"    a: &[f32], b: &[f32],\n"
"    mut c: DisjointSlice<f32, Runtime2DIndex>,\n"
") {\n"
"    static mut TILE_A: SharedArray<f32, 256> = SharedArray::UNINIT;\n"
"    static mut TILE_B: SharedArray<f32, 256> = SharedArray::UNINIT;\n"
"\n"
"    let n_sz = n as usize;\n"
"    let row = thread::index_2d_row();\n"
"    let col = thread::index_2d_col();\n"
"    let tx = thread::threadIdx_x() as usize;\n"
"    let ty = thread::threadIdx_y() as usize;\n"
"\n"
"    let mut sum = 0.0f32;\n"
"    let mut t = 0u32;\n"
"\n"
"    while t < k / TILE as u32 {\n"
"        let tile_offset = t as usize * TILE;\n"
"\n"
"        // Phase 1: cooperative load\n"
"        unsafe {\n"
"            TILE_A[ty * TILE + tx] = a[row * k as usize + tile_offset + "
"tx];\n"
"            TILE_B[ty * TILE + tx] = b[(tile_offset + ty) * n_sz + col];\n"
"        }\n"
"\n"
"        // All threads must finish loading before any thread reads\n"
"        thread::sync_threads();\n"
"\n"
"        // Phase 2: compute from shared memory\n"
"        let mut i = 0usize;\n"
"        while i < TILE {\n"
"            unsafe {\n"
"                sum += TILE_A[ty * TILE + i] * TILE_B[i * TILE + tx];\n"
"            }\n"
"            i += 1;\n"
"        }\n"
"\n"
"        // Must sync before overwriting the tile in the next iteration\n"
"        thread::sync_threads();\n"
"\n"
"        t += 1;\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    // The row width comes from `c`, bound on the host to this same `n`.\n"
"    if let Some(c_idx) = thread::index_2d_runtime(&c) {\n"
"        if let Some(c_elem) = c.get_mut(c_idx) {\n"
"            *c_elem = sum;\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:115
msgid "Declaration rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:117
msgid ""
"`SharedArray` must be declared as `static mut` inside the kernel function "
"body. This tells the compiler to allocate it in PTX shared address space (`."
"shared`). The `UNINIT` constant skips initialization — the contents are "
"undefined until threads write to them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid "Parameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid "Meaning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid "`T`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid "Element type (`f32`, `u32`, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid "`N`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid "Number of elements (fixed at compile time)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid "`ALIGN`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:38
msgid ""
"Byte alignment: 0 (natural) or a power of two; use 128 for TMA destinations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:129
msgid ""
"`SharedArray` is `!Sync` by design — it wraps `UnsafeCell`, which prevents "
"the compiler from assuming immutability across threads. This is correct: "
"shared memory is inherently mutable by all threads in the block, and the "
"programmer is responsible for synchronization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:135
msgid "Alignment rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:137
msgid ""
"`ALIGN` is `0` (natural alignment) or a non-zero power of two. Any other "
"value is a compile error that names the use site."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:140
msgid ""
"Storage is never declared below `align_of::<T>()`. A smaller request is "
"raised silently, the way `#[repr(align(N))]` raises a struct; a larger "
"request is honored. `SharedArray<u64, 2, 1>` is therefore declared at 8 "
"bytes, and a `#[repr(C, align(16))]` element at natural alignment is "
"declared at 16. Without the raise, a `u64` array declared at 1 byte could be "
"packed right behind a byte array and fault with `misaligned address`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:147
msgid ""
"Aggregate, nested-array and `f16` elements at natural alignment declare "
"their natural alignment in PTX (`.align 4` for `[f32; N]`, `.align 2` for "
"`f16`). Older releases printed a blanket `.align 8` for them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:155
msgid ""
"Shared-memory storage alignment. The verifier rejects an `ALIGN` that is not "
"a non-zero power of two at the use site, and lowering declares every static "
"array and the dynamic pool at the larger of the request and the element's "
"ABI alignment. `SharedArray<u64, 2, 1>` is declared `.align 8` and a "
"`#[repr(C, align(16))]` element at natural alignment `.align 16`, where "
"older releases declared `.align 1` and `.align 8` and the kernel could fault "
"with `misaligned address`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:162
msgid "The API surface"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:164
msgid ""
"impl<T, const N: usize, const ALIGN: usize> SharedArray<T, N, ALIGN> {\n"
"    pub const UNINIT: Self;\n"
"    pub const fn len() -> usize;\n"
"    pub fn as_ptr(&self) -> *const T;\n"
"    pub fn as_mut_ptr(&mut self) -> *mut T;\n"
"    pub unsafe fn as_raw_mut_ptr(shared: *mut Self) -> *mut T;\n"
"}\n"
"\n"
"// Indexing (unsafe access via static mut)\n"
"impl Index<usize> for SharedArray<T, N, ALIGN> { ... }\n"
"impl IndexMut<usize> for SharedArray<T, N, ALIGN> { ... }\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:178
msgid ""
"Indexing is bounds-checked in debug builds. In release (and on the GPU), the "
"bounds check is elided. If you index out of bounds, you get undefined "
"behavior — the same rules as any `static mut` access in Rust."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:184
msgid "DynamicSharedArray — runtime-sized allocation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:186
msgid ""
"Sometimes you do not know the tile size at compile time, or you want to "
"share a single shared memory pool across multiple logical arrays. "
"`DynamicSharedArray` allocates from the dynamic shared memory region, whose "
"size is set at launch time via `LaunchConfig::shared_mem_bytes`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:191
msgid ""
"use cuda_device::{DisjointSlice, DynamicSharedArray, kernel, thread};\n"
"\n"
"#[kernel]\n"
"pub fn reduce_dynamic(input: &[f32], n: u32, mut output: DisjointSlice<f32>) "
"{\n"
"    let tid = thread::threadIdx_x() as usize;\n"
"\n"
"    // Get a pointer to the dynamic shared memory region\n"
"    let smem: *mut f32 = DynamicSharedArray::<f32>::get();\n"
"\n"
"    unsafe {\n"
"        // Load from global\n"
"        let idx = thread::index_1d();\n"
"        *smem.add(tid) = if idx.get() < n as usize {\n"
"            input[idx.get()]\n"
"        } else {\n"
"            0.0\n"
"        };\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    thread::sync_threads();\n"
"\n"
"    // Tree reduction\n"
"    let mut stride = thread::blockDim_x() as usize / 2;\n"
"    while stride > 0 {\n"
"        if tid < stride {\n"
"            unsafe {\n"
"                *smem.add(tid) += *smem.add(tid + stride);\n"
"            }\n"
"        }\n"
"        thread::sync_threads();\n"
"        stride /= 2;\n"
"    }\n"
"\n"
"    if tid == 0 {\n"
"        let block_idx = thread::blockIdx_x() as usize;\n"
"        unsafe {\n"
"            *output.get_unchecked_mut(block_idx) = *smem;\n"
"        }\n"
"    }\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:234
msgid "Launch with:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:236
msgid ""
"let config = LaunchConfig {\n"
"    grid_dim: ((n + 255) / 256, 1, 1),\n"
"    block_dim: (256, 1, 1),\n"
"    shared_mem_bytes: 256 * std::mem::size_of::<f32>() as u32,\n"
"};\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:244
msgid "Partitioning dynamic shared memory"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:246
msgid "If you need multiple arrays from the same dynamic pool, use `offset()`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:248
msgid ""
"let pool_a: *mut f32 = DynamicSharedArray::<f32>::get();\n"
"let pool_b: *mut f32 = DynamicSharedArray::<f32>::offset(\n"
"    256 * std::mem::size_of::<f32>()\n"
");\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:255
msgid ""
"`offset` takes a **byte offset** from the start of the pool. Make sure the "
"total does not exceed `shared_mem_bytes` — there are no runtime guards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:258
msgid "Alignment"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:260
msgid ""
"`DynamicSharedArray` defaults to 16-byte alignment. For TMA operations "
"(Hopper+), use `DynamicSharedArray<f32, 128>` to get the required 128-byte "
"alignment. The alignment is encoded in the PTX `.align` directive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:264
msgid ""
"`ALIGN` must be a non-zero power of two; `0` is a compile error that names "
"the use site. A kernel has one dynamic pool, and the compiler declares it at "
"the strongest typed requirement over the kernel's `get`, `get_raw` and "
"`offset` uses, each taken as the larger of its `ALIGN` and `align_of::"
"<T>()`, combined with the launch contract's alignment. `DynamicSharedArray::"
"<u64, 1>::get()` alone declares the pool at 8 bytes. The declaration aligns "
"the pool base only; keeping an `offset(byte_offset)` result aligned for `T` "
"is still the caller's job."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:275
msgid "Synchronization: sync_threads()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:277
msgid ""
"`thread::sync_threads()` is a **block-wide barrier**. It compiles to PTX "
"`bar.sync 0` and guarantees two things:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:280
msgid ""
"**All threads in the block** have reached the barrier before any proceed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:281
msgid ""
"**All memory writes** by those threads are visible to all threads after the "
"barrier (it acts as a memory fence for shared memory)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:284
msgid ""
"Without `sync_threads()` between the load and compute phases, some threads "
"might read a shared memory location before another thread has written it. "
"The hardware does not guarantee any ordering within a warp for shared memory "
"stores — even threads in the same warp can see stale values without a "
"barrier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:290
msgid "When to sync"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:292
msgid "The rule is simple: **sync before reading what another thread wrote**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid "Situation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid "Need sync?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid "Thread A writes `TILE[i]`, Thread B reads `TILE[i]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "Yes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid "Thread A writes `TILE[i]`, Thread A reads `TILE[i]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid "No (same thread)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid "Overwriting a tile for the next loop iteration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid ""
"Yes (before the new load overwrites data threads might still be reading)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
msgid "Reading from `DisjointSlice` (each thread reads its own index)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:151
#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "No"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:301
msgid ""
"The tiled GEMM above has two sync points per iteration: one after loading "
"(before computing) and one after computing (before the next load overwrites "
"the tile). Missing either one is a data race."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:306
msgid ""
"A common mistake is putting `sync_threads()` inside a conditional branch "
"that not all threads take. Every thread in the block must reach the same "
"`sync_threads()` call, or the kernel will deadlock. If you need divergent "
"control flow, restructure so the barrier is outside the branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:314
msgid "Compiler ordering around the barrier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:316
msgid ""
"The hardware guarantee above covers visibility between threads. A second "
"question is whether the compiler keeps your own accesses on the side of the "
"barrier where you wrote them. Both backends emit the barrier as an operation "
"that the compiler treats as reading and writing all memory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:321
msgid ""
"The LLVM NVPTX route calls `llvm.nvvm.barrier.cta.sync.aligned.all`. The "
"call is `convergent` and has no `memory(...)` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:323
msgid ""
"The libNVVM route emits `bar.sync 0;` as `asm sideeffect` with a `~{memory}` "
"clobber."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "Access form"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "Kept on its side of `sync_threads()`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "`SharedArray` indexing, `S[i]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "A plain load or store in address space 3."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "`SharedArray::as_raw_mut_ptr(&raw mut S)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"The same instructions as indexing. Prefer it to `as_mut_ptr()` and "
"`as_ptr()`. Those methods borrow `S`, and the `static_mut_refs` lint rejects "
"a borrow of a `static mut` under edition 2024."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "`DynamicSharedArray::<T>::get()` and `offset()`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "A plain load or store through the returned pointer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "`ptx_asm!` with no options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"The call is `asm sideeffect` with no memory attribute. The PTX keeps it in "
"order against the barrier, plain loads and stores, and other side-effecting "
"asm. ptxas may drop a snippet with an unused result from the SASS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "`ptx_asm!` with `clobber(\"memory\")`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"Adds `~{memory}` to the constraint string. The order matches the row above "
"in the current lowering. Without the clobber that order comes from the "
"`sideeffect` flag alone. That flag does not tell the compiler that the "
"snippet touches memory. Add it to every snippet that loads or stores through "
"an address."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "`ptx_asm!` with `options(register_only)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"The call has no `sideeffect` flag. The compiler may move it across memory "
"operations and the barrier, and deletes it when its result is unused. Use it "
"for snippets that touch registers only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"`cp_async_*` copies, `cp_async_commit_group()`, `cp_async_wait_group(n)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "Yes, but see below"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"Issue, commit and wait stay in order. The barrier does not wait for the copy "
"to land."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"A `&T` or `&[T]` to slots another thread writes, live across the barrier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "rustc merges reads through it before either backend runs. See below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "A `&UnsafeCell<T>` to such a slot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"A plain load that is re-read after each barrier. Two adjacent reads with "
"nothing between them may still become one load."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid "A reference to a `BlockAtomic*` type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:305
msgid ""
"Each `load()` is side-effecting asm with a memory clobber. The compiler "
"keeps every one."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:339
msgid ""
"The indexing, `as_raw_mut_ptr`, `DynamicSharedArray` and both side-effecting "
"`ptx_asm!` rows were checked on both backends with a producer and consumer "
"kernel: store, barrier, load, barrier, store, barrier, load. The PTX keeps "
"that order for each of them. It keeps the same order when the loads sit in a "
"loop whose trip count is a kernel argument. A run on an RTX 5060 Laptop "
"(sm_120) read the expected neighbour values. A `register_only` `ld.shared` "
"whose result is unused leaves no instruction in the PTX. The same snippet "
"with no options stays. The `cp.async` and reference rows have their own "
"tests below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:348
msgid ""
"The table covers compiler ordering only. The rule under \"When to sync\" "
"still decides where the barriers belong."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:351
msgid "Raw pointers across the barrier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:353
msgid ""
"Each thread derives its pointer from the raw address of the allocation and "
"creates no reference to the whole array. A thread writes its own slot before "
"the barrier and reads a neighbour's slot after it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:357
msgid ""
"static mut TILE: SharedArray<u32, 128> = SharedArray::UNINIT;\n"
"\n"
"let tid = thread::threadIdx_x() as usize;\n"
"let tile = unsafe { SharedArray::as_raw_mut_ptr(&raw mut TILE) };\n"
"unsafe { tile.add(tid).write(tid as u32 + 1) }; // this thread's slot\n"
"thread::sync_threads();\n"
"let next = unsafe { tile.add((tid + 1) % 128).read() }; // a neighbour's "
"slot\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:367
msgid ""
"A kernel built from this snippet read the neighbour's value in all 128 "
"threads on both backends."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:370
msgid "`cp.async` needs a wait before the barrier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:372
msgid ""
"`bar.sync` does not wait for a pending `cp.async`. Each thread waits for its "
"own copies and then every thread meets at the barrier:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:375
msgid ""
"unsafe {\n"
"    cp_async_ca_4(dst, src);\n"
"    cp_async_commit_group();\n"
"    cp_async_wait_group(0); // this thread's copies have landed\n"
"}\n"
"thread::sync_threads(); // other threads may now read `dst`\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:384
msgid ""
"Reading the destination before the copy completes is undefined behaviour. A "
"test kernel that drops `cp_async_wait_group(0)` and reads a neighbour's slot "
"after the barrier can return the stale value: in a plain run on sm_120 all "
"128 threads did, and under compute-sanitizer every copy had landed first. "
"The same kernel with the wait returned the copied value in all 128 threads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:390
msgid "Share a slot through a reference only with `UnsafeCell`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:392
msgid ""
"When `T` has no interior mutability, a `&T` into shared memory tells rustc "
"that the target does not change while the reference is live. rustc merges "
"two reads through it into one load before either backend runs. In a test "
"kernel each thread read its neighbour's slot through a `&u32`, passed two "
"barriers while the neighbour wrote a new value, and read again. The second "
"read returned the old value in all 128 threads on both backends. A `&[T]` "
"built over the tile with `from_raw_parts` is the same case: two indexed "
"reads across the barrier compile to one load. Keeping such a reference live "
"across a barrier while another thread writes the target is undefined "
"behaviour. So is a `&mut T` to such a slot: the other thread's write breaks "
"its exclusive access, even though no pass merges reads through it today."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../advanced/shared-memory-and-synchronization.md:404
msgid ""
"A `&UnsafeCell<T>` is re-read after each barrier, and every `BlockAtomic*` "
"load is an instruction the compiler keeps. The same kernel with either form "
"read the new value in all 128 threads. Share a slot through one of these, or "
"read through a raw pointer after each barrier. The tip under \"Declaration "
"rules\" gives the same reason for `SharedArray` wrapping `UnsafeCell`."
msgstr ""

